Problem Statement

Given an array of integers nums and an integer k, return the total number of subarrays whose sum equals to k.
A subarray is a contiguous non-empty sequence of elements within an array.
 
Example 1:
Input: nums = [1,1,1], k = 2
Output: 2
Example 2:
Input: nums = [1,2,3], k = 3
Output: 2
 
1.Intuition
Let S[i] be the prefix sum of the array from index 0 to i:
S[i]=∑_(j=0)^i▒"nums"  [j]
The sum of elements in a subarray starting at index j and ending at index i (j≤i) is given by:
"Subarray Sum" (j,i)=S[i]-S[j-1]
We want to find pairs (j,i) such that:
S[i]-S[j-1]=k⟹S[j-1]=S[i]-k
This means as we iterate through the array maintaining the current running sum (S[i]), we simply need to check how many times the prefix sum (S[i]-k) has appeared before. A hash map allows us to store and lookup prefix sum frequencies in O(1) constant time.

2. Approach
1. Initialize a Hash Map: Map stores prefix sums as key and their frequency of occurrence as value: map<prefix_sum, count>.
2. Base Case: Initialize map[0] = 1. This accounts for subarrays starting at index 0 whose sum directly equals k (i.e., S[i]-k=0).
3.  Iterate & Count:
	Maintain a running prefix_sum.
	For each element, add it to prefix_sum.
	Check if (prefix_sum - k) exists in the hash map. If it does, add its count to the answer.
	Increment the frequency of the current prefix_sum in the hash map.
Complexity Analysis
	Time Complexity: O(n) — We perform a single pass over the array of length n. Each hash map insertion and lookup takes O(1) average time complexity.
	Space Complexity: O(n) — In the worst-case scenario (all prefix sums are distinct), the hash map will store up to n+1 elements.

3.Code
class Solution {
public:
    int subarraySum(std::vector<int>& nums, int k) {
        std::unordered_map<int, int> prefixSumCount;        
        // Base case: prefix sum of 0 occurs once (before starting the array)
        prefixSumCount[0] = 1;
        int currentPrefixSum = 0;
        int count = 0;
        for (int num : nums) {
            currentPrefixSum += num;
            // If (currentPrefixSum - k) exists in hash map, add its frequency
            if (prefixSumCount.find(currentPrefixSum - k) != prefixSumCount.end()) {
                count += prefixSumCount[currentPrefixSum - k];
            }
               prefixSumCount[currentPrefixSum]++;
        }
        return count;
    }  };

